Async proxying and MPM queuing: benchmark harness and checks (prototype)

Build the Go tools (Go >= 1.21, no module needed):
  go build -o backend backend.go     # backend: GET /?d=MS answers 512 bytes after MS ms,
                                     # "Upgrade: echo" gets a 101 then an echo,
                                     # GET /?s=BYTES streams BYTES, POST reads its body
  go build -o loadgen loadgen.go     # load generator (closed loop, or open loop -rate;
                                     # paced uploads -upload, slow reads -rchunk)
  go build -o tunnelgen tunnelgen.go # tunnels (Upgrade: echo) sending a message every -i
  go build -o fcgibackend fcgibackend.go # FastCGI backend: GET /?d=MS answers 512 bytes
                                     # after MS ms, /?s=BYTES streams BYTES, POST reads
                                     # its body; -raw ADDR misbehaves (closes early)
  go build -o schemebackend schemebackend.go # AJP13 (-ajp), SCGI (-scgi) and uwsgi
                                     # (-uwsgi) responders: ?d=MS, ?s=BYTES, ?close=0

Build mod_aptest (its "aptest-queue" handler, controlled service times):
  cd ../pyhttpd/mod_aptest && $PREFIX/bin/apxs -c mod_aptest.c

The scripts start their own httpd from an install prefix ($PREFIX, e.g. built
from this tree) with their own configuration under WORKDIR, on 127.0.0.1
ports 18431-18435 (checks) and 18500-18510 (bench, queue). They pin httpd on
CPUs 0-3 and the tools on 4-7 (taskset); for stable numbers use a fixed CPU
frequency (cpupower frequency-set -g performance, no turbo). CPUS="HTTPD
BACKEND TOOLS" changes the CPUs of the benches (e.g. CPUS="0-1 2 3" on 4
CPUs). The benches using bench.py's helpers and the checks also run on
FreeBSD (cpuset, ps, truss and netstat for taskset, /proc, strace and ss);
queue.py is Linux only.

bench.py PREFIX WORKDIR [delays] [backend_ms]
  ProxyAsyncDelay benchmark: blocking (-1) vs async (0, 1ms, 10ms) against
  backends answering in 0/1/10/100ms, 64 clients on one child of 8 threads;
  throughput, latency percentiles, CPU and context switches per request of
  the httpd tree. POOLMAX sets the backend pool's max= (256 by default, below
  CLIENTS for a pool-bound run where requests wait for a connection); STRACE=1
  adds the httpd child's syscalls per request (strace -c over the measured
  window, which slows it down: only the per-request ratios are meaningful).
  Build httpd optimized for numbers that mean something (-O2, no AP_DEBUG).
  Run it several times into log files, then medians.py LOG... for the median
  of each cell.

tunnelbench.py PREFIX WORKDIR [tunnels,...]
  Tunnel benchmark: 100, 1000 and 5000 tunnels by default (Upgrade: echo
  through mod_proxy_http, async), each sending a SIZE-byte message (64) every
  INTERVAL (1s) to the backend's echo; messages/s, latency percentiles, CPU
  and context switches per message, and syscalls with STRACE=1 (the
  epoll_ctl churn per message). DELAY=-1 for blocking tunnels (one thread
  each, so no more tunnels than ThreadsPerChild). medians.py reads its logs
  too.

bodybench.py PREFIX WORKDIR [delays] [scenarios]
  Large-body benchmark: blocking (-1) vs async (0) by default, 16 clients,
  responses of SIZE bytes (4MB) streamed by the backend in CHUNK-byte writes
  (64KB) and read by clients slower than the backend (down-slow: a CHUNK read
  every PAUSE, 1ms) or as fast as possible (down-fast), and uploads of SIZE
  bytes written a CHUNK every PAUSE (up). On loopback these hardly make a
  request wait more than once (large socket buffers), so down-crawl and
  up-crawl read or send CRAWL_SIZE bytes (1MB) a CRAWL_CHUNK (16KB) every
  CRAWL_PAUSE (20ms), down-crawl through the client's receive buffer and
  httpd's SendBufferSize set to CRAWL_CHUNK: about 60 waits per request.
  Requests/s, MB/s, latency percentiles, CPU and context switches per MB of
  the httpd tree, and syscalls per request with STRACE=1. medians.py reads its
  logs too.

queue.py PREFIX MOD_APTEST_SO WORKDIR [scenario...]
  MPM queuing scenarios (burst, overload-cpu, blocked, async-mix, progress,
  two-children): a background load and open-loop probes (a new connection
  each, latency from the scheduled time) against "aptest-queue", with the
  server's backlog stats (mpm_event's "backlog sojourn" lines, LogLevel
  mpm_event:trace2) aggregated over the measured window and httpd's CPU use.
  The admission's target and interval (ms, 5 and 100 by default) are set at
  build time (-DAP_EVENT_ADMISSION_TARGET=, -DAP_EVENT_ADMISSION_INTERVAL=).

checks/async-*-check.py PREFIX WORKDIR
  Functional checks of the async proxy paths, each comparing blocking ("sync")
  and async (ProxyAsyncDelay 0) modes (MODES="sync async" by default):
  proto (response wait), body (response body), send (request body), head
  (response head), connect, pool (waiting for a connection of an exhausted
  pool, max= and acquire=), prefetch (request body before acquiring), tls
  (backend handshake), remote (the CONNECT exchange with a forward proxy,
  ProxyRemote, in front of the tls backend), balancer (failover,
  failonstatus/failontimeout), shutdown (graceful and hard restarts/stops
  with suspended requests), exit (httpd -X stopped with a request suspended,
  its pools destroyed at exit), 100 (Expect: 100-continue end-to-end, ping=, ping
  timeout, final responses instead of 100, the 100 sent by httpd itself, and
  uploads waiting for the 100 not holding a worker), tunnel (Upgrade: tunnels
  exchanging data, many on few threads, the idle timeout, either side
  closing, a large transfer to a slow client, Upgrades answered late or
  refused; tunnels being async by default, "sync" is ProxyAsyncDelay -1
  there, and the "default", "wstunnel" (mod_proxy_wstunnel) and "connect"
  (mod_proxy_connect) modes are added), fcgi (mod_proxy_fcgi against
  fcgibackend: waits on few threads, timeout=, large streamed responses,
  request bodies, the backend closing early or down, pipelined requests,
  backend keep-alive with disablereuse=off, flushes (flushpackets=on) and an
  error's ErrorDocument after an async wait to clients that stop reading),
  redirect (an ErrorDocument proxied over http and fcgi: internally
  redirected requests don't suspend; and after an async wait, proxied to the
  same backend reusing its connection), schemes (mod_proxy_ajp,
  mod_proxy_scgi and mod_proxy_uwsgi against schemebackend: waits on few
  threads, timeout=, large responses, request bodies, early close, backend
  down, a pool of one connection), balancer-wait (requests waiting for a
  usable member until the balancer's timeout=, the backend coming back or
  not), tlsfront (mod_ssl's client connections: silent clients timing out in
  the handshake, clients not reading the server's handshake flight, slow TLS
  downloads of a large static file, a TLS backend not reading the proxy's
  handshake flight, none holding a worker).
  PASSOPTS adds ProxyPass options (e.g. max=32 to not be bounded by the
  backend connection pool).

checks/async-wait-check.py PREFIX [MOD_APTEST_SO] WORKDIR
  The core's request waits (ap_request_wait()) without mod_proxy, through
  mod_aptest's "aptest-wait" handler (built with the install's apxs into
  WORKDIR unless MOD_APTEST_SO is given): many timers on few threads,
  crawling uploads (1MB, 16KB every 20ms) and crawling readers of an echo
  (small socket buffers), chunked uploads whose trailers trickle, the read
  timeouts (Timeout, and RequestReadTimeout's body= winning over it), a TLS
  upload cut in the middle of a record, clients aborting mid-upload, a
  notifier and its context serving several waits (re-armed, a stale
  notification dropped, a re-arm in flight refused, a wait without the
  re-arm declined and logged), several waits of a request pending at once
  (a notifier, two descriptor contexts on one pipe, two timers completing
  together and called in the order they were made, a wait canceled, one
  dropped by the request's end; no worker held, 16 requests at once; a
  subrequest run by the notifier's continuation while the others are
  pending: suspended with them), the MPM's keep and drained
  flags of each descriptor of a wait (two pipes, five MPM waits, from
  mpm_event's trace7 lines for a connection from 127.0.0.2), 200
  descriptor contexts per request made in subpools destroyed after their
  waits, and a graceful stop during waits;
  "sync" is AsyncRequests Off (every wait
  blocks: the same results, and the blocking timers: as many as the threads
  holding them all, as many more as wait for a worker without being closed
  by Timeout, then beyond: some closed, the accepting stopped, the server
  serving after), "async" also checks that no worker is held (mod_status)
  and the times.

checks/async-resume-check.py PREFIX [MOD_APTEST_SO] WORKDIR
  Handler chains resumed after a suspension (ap_request_resume_suspended()),
  through mod_aptest's AptestSuspend handlers acting before the others
  (mod_aptest built as for async-wait-check): suspended then DECLINED to a
  static file or to mod_proxy (suspending in turn, backend's -addr on port
  18448), two suspending handlers in a row, a handler answering, a 403 with
  a local or a proxied ErrorDocument, the output filters answering an error
  (one response), a resume before the handler returned SUSPENDED (refused
  and logged; a debug build asserts, the child aborts), the suspending
  location in a subrequest (mod_include), pipelined requests behind
  suspended ones, many clients on few threads, ExtendedStatus' Total Duration
  (no time counted while suspended in "async") and a graceful stop during
  suspensions; "sync" is AsyncRequests Off (the handlers block: the same
  responses), "async" also checks that no worker is held (mod_status).

checks/async-reqbody-check.py PREFIX [MOD_APTEST_SO] WORKDIR
  The core's request body reader (ap_request_read_body(),
  ap_request_wait_body(), ap_request_body_run(), ap_request_discard_body())
  through mod_aptest's "aptest-body" handler (AptestBody pull, sink,
  discard; mod_aptest built as for async-wait-check) and AptestSuspend's
  "read": Content-Length and chunked bodies, small and 8MB (digests),
  crawling uploads on few threads, trickling trailers, Expect: 100-continue,
  LimitRequestBody (413 by Content-Length and mid-body), the reader's 408
  (Timeout) and mod_reqtimeout's (RequestReadTimeout body=), a malformed
  chunk, a TLS upload cut in a record, clients aborting mid-upload, the
  discard (a small leftover keeping the connection, over the limit by
  Content-Length or chunked, a 100 Continue never sent), a body read then
  replayed to mod_proxy_http (this server's /pull, on port 18452),
  mod_proxy_scgi and mod_proxy_uwsgi (schemebackend on ports 18453-18454),
  blocking reads from an output filter (AP_REQUEST_BODY_BLOCK), the error
  log, and graceful and immediate stops during uploads; "sync" is
  AsyncRequests Off (the reads block: the same responses), "async" also
  checks that no worker is held (mod_status). Ports 18449-18454.

checks/async-subreq-check.py PREFIX [MOD_APTEST_SO] WORKDIR
  Subrequests run asynchronously (ap_run_sub_req_async()) through
  mod_aptest's AptestSubreq handler acting before the others (mod_aptest
  built as for async-wait-check), their output passed to the client or
  captured by an output filter of the subrequest: proxied subrequests
  (backend's -addr on port 18456) suspended while mod_proxy waits, 1, 3 and
  8 per request (8 streamed ones: the backend's descriptor kept by the MPM,
  never more than EVENT_KEPT_MAX), subrequests suspended by AptestSuspend
  then resumed to a static file, a nested subrequest, DECLINED to a static
  file after the subrequests, a subrequest reaching its timeout (504 to the
  caller, "async" only: blocking subrequests have no deadline), pipelined
  requests behind requests running subrequests, many clients on few threads,
  mod_auth_form's replay of a login form's request (the subrequest suspended
  by AptestSuspend), the error log and a graceful stop during subrequests;
  then mod_include's include virtual of a proxied URL, from its filter with
  the filter's hold delegated to the subrequest (no worker held in
  "async"), and two includes in a document, the second one's onerror run
  after it fails, with the same output.
  "sync" is AsyncRequests Off (the subrequests block: the same responses),
  "async" also checks that no worker is held (mod_status). Ports
  18455-18456.

checks/async-hold-check.py PREFIX [MOD_APTEST_SO] WORKDIR
  Request filters holding their output or input (ap_filter_pending_wait())
  through mod_aptest's AptestHold filters (mod_aptest built as for
  async-wait-check): a static file's EOS held on a timer and on a notifier
  by 16 clients on 4 threads, each chunk of a proxied stream held (the
  backend's -addr on port 18462), a 1MB response passed in pieces held for
  room to crawling readers, ap_die()'s 404 held by a protocol filter (the
  second hold point), request bodies held by an input filter while the core's
  body reader and mod_proxy wait for the client (completed by the hold, with
  nothing more on the socket), holds refused while a subrequest writes
  through the filter, pipelined requests, TLS, a hold longer than Timeout
  (given up, the connection closed), the core's invariants in the error log
  and a graceful stop while holding. "sync" is AsyncRequests Off (the holds
  refused, the filter sleeping: the same responses), "async" also checks
  that no worker is held (mod_status). Ports 18460-18462.

checks/async-filter-subreq-check.py PREFIX [MOD_APTEST_SO] WORKDIR
  Request filters delegating their holds to subrequests
  (AP_FILTER_HOLD_DELEGATED, then ap_run_sub_req_async()) through mod_aptest's
  AptestFilterSubreq filter (mod_aptest built as for async-wait-check): 1, 3
  and 8 proxied subrequests in turn at a static file's EOS (captured, or
  passed below the filter), 3 before the first data of 8 proxied streams on
  4 threads (mid-stream, the producer paused), 16 clients on 4 threads, a
  subrequest reaching its timeout (504), the work's timeout bounding the
  request's end rather than Timeout (a subrequest whose own timeout is
  longer than Timeout completing past it, one without a timeout getting
  Timeout: 504), ap_die()'s 404 held by a protocol filter running one (the
  second hold point), a subrequest's own filter (refused, blocking), the
  core's checks of a filter running ap_run_sub_req_async() without a
  delegated hold (run blocking, logged) and of a delegated hold without its
  work (released, logged), pipelined requests, the core's invariants in the
  error log and a graceful stop.
  "sync" is AsyncRequests Off (the holds refused, the subrequests blocking:
  the same responses), "async" also checks that no worker is held
  (mod_status). Ports 18465-18466 (the backend's -addr on 18466).

checks/async-pace-check.py PREFIX WORKDIR
  Paced output (ap_filter_pending_until()): mod_ratelimit (rate-limit
  200KB/s) pacing a 200KB static file and a proxied stream for 8 clients on
  4 threads (the rate kept, no worker held), a FLUSH reaching it (mod_proxy
  streaming blocking with ProxyAsyncDelay -1: it sleeps as before),
  mod_dialup's pulses a second apart (V.92) on timer waits, pipelined paced
  responses in order, and the core's invariants in the error log. "sync" is
  AsyncRequests Off (the filters sleep: the same bytes and rates), "async"
  also checks that no worker is held (mod_status). Ports 18463-18464 (the
  backend's -addr on 18464).

checks/async-tunnel-hold-check.py PREFIX [MOD_APTEST_SO] WORKDIR
  Tunnels held by their forward hooks (ap_proxy_tunnel_hold()) through
  mod_aptest's AptestTunnelHold hook (mod_aptest built as for
  async-wait-check), on mod_proxy_http's Upgrade tunnels to an echo backend
  sending ticks or not: 8 tunnels on 2 threads holding each message 300ms,
  the ticks flowing while the client's message is held (and not with
  "pause"), a hold longer than the idle timeout (each direction on its own
  idle clock: the other direction quiet ends the tunnel at the idle
  timeout, flowing it keeps the tunnel until both are idle), a service's
  answer timing out (APR_TIMEUP to the hook), a hold without timeout
  bounded by the idle timeout, a delegated subrequest per message (proxied,
  the ticks flowing meanwhile; static, ended at once), a TLS front with two
  messages decrypted at once, holds on sockets in both directions (no
  EVENT_KEPT_MAX line) and a graceful stop while held. "sync"
  is ProxyAsyncDelay -1 (a synchronous tunnel: the holds refused, the hook
  working blocking: the same bytes), "async" also checks that no worker is
  held (mod_status), the times and the ticks. Ports 18467-18471 (the
  backend's -addr on 18471).

checks/h1-head-check.py PREFIX WORKDIR
  The HTTP/1 head rules on both sides (ap_h1_head_parse()): request heads
  from a client (HttpProtocolOptions Strict and Unsafe, LimitRequestLine,
  LimitRequestFieldSize, LimitRequestFields: line ends, NUL bytes, field
  names and values, obs-folds, the limits at limit + 1 and + 2, empty lines
  before the request line, HTTP/0.9, the client closing), and response heads
  and trailers from a raw backend through mod_proxy_http
  (ProxyHttpProtocolOptions Strict and Unsafe, the ProxyLimitResponse*
  limits: the same rules, stray CRLFs, malformed status lines, interim
  responses, the backend closing), each head sent whole, one byte per packet
  and split in two at every offset (SPLITS="whole byte every"), the cause of
  each error logged; the response heads in MODES="sync async", the request
  heads in the first mode.
